		heading: 'What “Abba” means',
		bodyHtml: `<p><em>Abba</em> is an Aramaic word meaning <strong>&ldquo;Father&rdquo;</strong> &mdash; the everyday word a child used at home for the father he trusted. Aramaic was the ordinary spoken language of first-century Galilee and Judea, the language Jesus used in daily life, and <em>Abba</em> belonged to the household rather than the temple.</p><p>It is often explained as &ldquo;Daddy,&rdquo; which captures the warmth but overstates the babyishness: grown sons and daughters used it of their fathers too. The better sense is <em>the name you call your father when he is genuinely yours</em> &mdash; intimate and unguarded, but not childish.</p><p>That is why the New Testament keeps the Aramaic word instead of translating it away. The three passages where it appears all add the Greek word for Father straight after it &mdash; <em>Abba, Father</em> &mdash; as if the writers wanted Greek readers to feel the original word and understand it at once.</p>`,
